<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

    <PropertyGroup>
        <LangVersion>default</LangVersion>
        <PackageId>Pooshit.Http</PackageId>
        <Version>0.15.1-preview</Version>
        <Title>Pooshit.Http</Title>
        <Authors>Toni Gottschall</Authors>
        <Description>Library simplifying http request handling</Description>
        <PackageReleaseNotes>
HttpService now treats only 200 to 299 as success, where it accepted 200 to 399 before, so a redirect that nobody followed raises HttpServiceException instead of being read as an empty success. This is the first of the two changes in this release and it changes what every caller sees from a redirect, whether or not they have configured anything, so read it before upgrading. Start with the calls that return nothing - Get(url), Post(url, body), Put, Patch, Delete, Request and Send without a type parameter - because those are the ones which have had no symptom at all. A redirect answered one of those and the call returned normally, with no value to be null and nothing downstream to trip over, so a resource that had moved was indistinguishable from a resource that had been updated. Those members run the status check directly and they now raise. Everywhere else the defect has been announcing itself, just not truthfully. A typed call returned null for the resource having moved, which is the same null an empty 200 or a 204 produces - that is measured, and it is why the status check had to move rather than the guard which turns an empty body into the default of the requested type - and a caller who used that null usually arrived at a NullReferenceException several frames from the cause. If you are currently receiving null from a 3xx, you are receiving a redirect. Nothing that works today stops working, with one exception: a conditional request that sends If-None-Match and reads a typed result was correct to treat null as not-modified, and it now raises - the 304 paragraph below is about that caller. Every other call this change makes raise was already failing, and what changes is that the failure stops lying about what it is. Redirect following is off by default and the platform handler is built with its own following off, so this was never an opt-in path. The exception names the redirect target beside the status, under the same dump rules as the header block below it, so its query is redacted the way every other url the library puts in a message is, a Location the caller has declared sensitive is withheld entirely, and a caller who set HeaderDumpMode.Omitted reads a redirect to an undisclosed location rather than the url. It names the two ways to handle it either way - set HttpOptions.FollowRedirects to have the library follow the redirect, or ask for HttpResponseMessage, which is exempt from status validation by design and hands back the live response for you to read. 304 Not Modified raises as well, and that is a decision rather than an oversight: a caller who read that null as not-modified was right about this response and wrong about the next one, because the same null arrives from an empty 200, and a conditional request needs the ETag, the cache headers and the status rather than a typed body, all of which HttpResponseMessage carries. So do 300, 305 and every other 3xx, including the ones the redirect follower does not recognise - those returned a silent null even with FollowRedirects set, a hole the 307 and 308 work in 0.14.0-preview left open and this closes. A chain of two redirects under FollowRedirects raises now too: where the first is followed, the second reaches the status check. It is still not followed, because only one hop ever is, but it is no longer silent. No opt-out ships with this. The only honest setting for one would be the behaviour being removed, and the way out already exists and is complete for every status. Unaffected: every 2xx, including 204 and extension statuses such as 207 and 226, which still succeed and still return null for an empty body exactly as before; the 4xx and 5xx classification, which this change does not touch; a redirect this library follows, which this change does not reach, because the hop runs before the status check and the check sees the hop's response rather than the redirect that caused it; and Get&lt;HttpResponseMessage&gt; and Send&lt;HttpResponseMessage&gt;, which never ran the check and still do not. This change narrows one runtime difference rather than closing it: on WebAssembly the platform client follows redirects itself, so a 3xx is rarely seen there, while on every other runtime it now raises. Both are loud. They are still not the same.

HttpOptions.UrlProcessor now documents the contract it has always had, and no behaviour changes with it. This is the second of the two changes in this release and it is documentation only, so no call behaves differently after upgrading. The processor resolves the target of a redirect hop rather than rewriting one: it is called with the location the response named, or with null when the response named none, and a target it returns is used while a null it returns declines the hop, which is then handled exactly as though the response had named no target. It runs on the redirect path only and not on outbound urls generally, which the previous summary implied and which was never true of it, and it runs before the target is resolved against the request url, so what it returns decides the same origin question that governs whether the names in HttpService.SensitiveHeaders ride the hop. An exception it throws reaches the caller unchanged, and the superseded redirect response is disposed on the way out. Unaffected: every call, because this release changes no code on this path - the contract stated here is the one the library already implemented, and it is written down now because the signature Func of string to string cannot express it and this package does not enable nullable reference types.

One statement in the 0.14.0-preview notes is corrected by measurement rather than by a change, and nothing in this release behaves differently because of it. That note scoped the verb-preserving 307 and 308 hop to .NET Core and .NET 5 or later and described the .NET Framework path as unmeasured. It has since been measured, on .NET Framework 4.8 against the netstandard2.0 assembly. On .NET Framework this capability is absent rather than unmeasured: HttpClient disposes the request content once a send completes, so a body-carrying 307 or 308 raises HttpServiceException instead of being followed - and unlike on .NET Core, the body's shape makes no difference, because the content object itself is disposed rather than merely consumed, so a buffered body and a seekable stream fail exactly as a non-seekable one does. A redirect with no body still follows normally, and where a body-carrying hop cannot be followed the failure arrives at the first redirect rather than at the second. No code change was needed for this: the disposal surfaces as ObjectDisposedException, which derives from InvalidOperationException and is already the exception the hop catches and translates.
        </PackageReleaseNotes>
        <PackageTags>http</PackageTags>
        <Company></Company>
        <PackageLicenseFile>LICENSE</PackageLicenseFile>
        <PackageProjectUrl>https://github.com/telmengedar/Pooshit.Http</PackageProjectUrl>
        <RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/telmengedar/Pooshit.Http</RepositoryUrl>
        <TargetFrameworks>netstandard2.0;net8.0</TargetFrameworks>
        <Product>Pooshit.Http</Product>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <None Include="LICENSE" Pack="true" PackagePath="" />
    </ItemGroup>

